1a:["$","p","p-1",{"children":["A small state machine is enough: ",["$","code","code-0",{"children":"accepted → running → succeeded"}],", with ",["$","code","code-1",{"children":"failed"}]," and ",["$","code","code-2",{"children":"cancelled"}]," as explicit exits. ",["$","code","code-3",{"children":"GET /exports/{id}"}]," returns status, a safe error code, and a file URL only after success. An unknown ID is different from a known failed export. A request with the same idempotency key should return the existing operation rather than create another export."]}]
1b:["$","p","p-2",{"children":["The API can return ",["$","code","code-0",{"children":"202 Accepted"}]," with ",["$","code","code-1",{"children":"Location: /exports/{id}"}],". Return it only after the operation and a publishable intent are durable. If the database commit fails, return an error. If the queue publish is separate from the database commit, the outbox pattern in lesson 5 closes that gap."]}]
